1
00:00:01,703 --> 00:00:03,705
- ♪ In the
not too distant future ♪

2
00:00:03,772 --> 00:00:06,975
♪ Next Sunday A.D. ♪

3
00:00:07,042 --> 00:00:10,010
♪ There was a guy named Joel ♪

4
00:00:10,078 --> 00:00:12,413
♪˯ Not too different
from you or me ♪

5
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:16,117
♪ He worked
at Gizmonic Institute ♪

6
00:00:16,217 --> 00:00:19,120
♪ Just another face
in a red jumpsuit ♪

7
00:00:19,187 --> 00:00:22,523
♪ He did a good job
cleaning up the place ♪

8
00:00:22,590 --> 00:00:24,225
♪ But his bosses
didn't like him ♪

9
00:00:24,292 --> 00:00:26,093
♪ So they shot him
into space ♪

10
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:28,630
[ rocket rumbling ]

11
00:00:28,697 --> 00:00:31,833
♪ We'll send him
cheesy movies ♪

12
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:33,968
♪ The worst we can find ♪

13
00:00:34,035 --> 00:00:35,270
♪ La la la ♪

14
00:00:35,337 --> 00:00:37,838
♪ He’ll have to sit
and watch them all ♪

15
00:00:37,905 --> 00:00:40,007
♪ And we’ll monitor his mind ♪

16
00:00:40,075 --> 00:00:41,442
♪ La la la ♪

17
00:00:41,508 --> 00:00:44,412
♪ Now keep in mind
Joel can't control ♪

18
00:00:44,478 --> 00:00:46,380
♪ Where the movies
begin or end ♪

19
00:00:46,447 --> 00:00:47,648
♪ La la la ♪

20
00:00:47,715 --> 00:00:50,652
♪ Because he used
those special parts ♪

21
00:00:50,718 --> 00:00:53,522
♪ To make his robot friends ♪

22
00:00:55,457 --> 00:00:57,092
Robot roll call!

23
00:00:57,158 --> 00:00:58,392
- Cambot!
- Pan left!

24
00:00:58,460 --> 00:00:59,927
- Gypsy!
- Hi, girl!

25
00:00:59,993 --> 00:01:01,830
- Tom Servo!
- What a cool guy.

26
00:01:01,897 --> 00:01:03,532
- Croooow!
- Wisecracker!

27
00:01:03,598 --> 00:01:06,300
♪ If you’re wondering
how he eats and breathes ♪

28
00:01:06,367 --> 00:01:08,135
♪ And other science facts ♪

29
00:01:08,202 --> 00:01:09,503
♪ La la la ♪

30
00:01:09,570 --> 00:01:12,473
♪ Then repeat to yourself
it's just a show ♪

31
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:15,343
♪ I should
really just relax ♪

32
00:01:15,410 --> 00:01:20,715
♪ For Mystery
Science Theater 3000 ♪

33
00:01:30,958 --> 00:01:32,693
[ both laughing ]

34
00:01:34,495 --> 00:01:36,097
- Oh, what are you guys doing?

35
00:01:36,163 --> 00:01:38,567
You're supposed to be helping me
crunch numbers

36
00:01:38,633 --> 00:01:39,900
for that navigational
plot thing.

37
00:01:39,967 --> 00:01:41,602
- I’m not going back, Jim.

38
00:01:41,668 --> 00:01:43,705
- Oh, come on, not this again.

39
00:01:43,772 --> 00:01:45,240
You guys, I’ve been waiting.

40
00:01:45,307 --> 00:01:47,375
- I’m in love, Jim.
- Oh, you!

41
00:01:47,442 --> 00:01:49,410
- Hi, everybody. Welcome
to the Satellite of Love.

42
00:01:49,477 --> 00:01:50,412
I’m Joel Robinson.

43
00:01:50,478 --> 00:01:51,778
If you haven't
already noticed,

44
00:01:51,847 --> 00:01:53,648
my bots,
Tom Servo and Crow,

45
00:01:53,715 --> 00:01:56,183
seem to be obsessing
on the Elias Sandoval episode

46
00:01:56,250 --> 00:01:57,518
of Star Trek.

47
00:01:57,585 --> 00:02:00,053
- I like it here
on Omicron Seti III, Jim.

48
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:03,023
- It really is wonderful here,
Captain Kirk.

49
00:02:03,090 --> 00:02:05,427
- Five seconds
to commercial time.

50
00:02:05,493 --> 00:02:07,695
Can I be of any help, Joel?

51
00:02:07,762 --> 00:02:09,397
- No, I just have
to get them mad,

52
00:02:09,463 --> 00:02:11,465
and then they seem
to snap out of it.

53
00:02:11,532 --> 00:02:15,002
You’re incapable
of feeling love, Spock.

54
00:02:15,068 --> 00:02:17,405
- I would not say such things
if I were you, Jim.

55
00:02:17,472 --> 00:02:19,707
- You belong
in the circus, Spock,

56
00:02:19,773 --> 00:02:22,010
right next
to the dog-faced boy.

57
00:02:22,110 --> 00:02:23,443
- That’s enough, Captain.

58
00:02:23,477 --> 00:02:24,912
- We’ll be right back.

59
00:02:24,978 --> 00:02:26,982
[ both laughing ]

60
00:02:32,787 --> 00:02:34,588
- [ imitating William Shatner ]
But does she know

61
00:02:34,655 --> 00:02:36,057
what she’s getting, Spock?

62
00:02:36,123 --> 00:02:38,627
Your mother was a computer, and
your father was an encyclopedia.

63
00:02:38,693 --> 00:02:42,197
- My mother was a teacher
and my father an ambassador.

64
00:02:42,263 --> 00:02:43,632
- Knock it off, you two.

65
00:02:43,698 --> 00:02:45,098
Aunt Dan
and Lemon are calling.

66
00:02:45,165 --> 00:02:46,767
- [ hissing ]

67
00:02:46,833 --> 00:02:49,003
- [ on TV ] Now, remember,
Hobie, you stay away

68
00:02:49,070 --> 00:02:50,338
from the kids
down by the liquor store.

69
00:02:50,405 --> 00:02:51,338
[ changes channel ]

70
00:02:51,405 --> 00:02:52,673
- Hey!

71
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:54,442
- Baywatch can wait
for now, Frank.

72
00:02:54,475 --> 00:02:55,910
With the money we earn
from this week's invention,

73
00:02:55,977 --> 00:02:57,112
we won’t have
to watch Baywatch.

74
00:02:57,178 --> 00:02:58,512
We’ll be able to live it.

75
00:02:58,580 --> 00:03:00,848
- So that means I could be
just like David Hasselhoff.

76
00:03:00,915 --> 00:03:02,917
I better practice sucking
in my gut.

77
00:03:02,983 --> 00:03:04,385
- You’re one to talk,
eh, Frank?

78
00:03:04,452 --> 00:03:05,387
- [ laughing ]

79
00:03:05,453 --> 00:03:06,687
Hey!

80
00:03:06,753 --> 00:03:09,157
- Well, bubby,
our invention this week

81
00:03:09,223 --> 00:03:11,692
is based on
those new high-priced TVs

82
00:03:11,758 --> 00:03:14,228
that allow you to check out
several other channels

83
00:03:14,295 --> 00:03:16,597
on the small screens
while you watch

84
00:03:16,663 --> 00:03:17,998
one program
on the big screen.

85
00:03:18,065 --> 00:03:20,402
Our invention goes
that one better.

86
00:03:20,468 --> 00:03:22,970
You see, it actually allows you
to check out and see

87
00:03:23,037 --> 00:03:25,307
what you would be doing
in your life at that moment

88
00:03:25,373 --> 00:03:27,675
if you weren’t
at home watching TV.

89
00:03:27,742 --> 00:03:29,210
It’s called the U-View.

90
00:03:29,277 --> 00:03:30,378
Check it out, Frank.

91
00:03:34,648 --> 00:03:36,183
[ changes channel ]

92
00:03:36,250 --> 00:03:39,087
- Hey, it's a beautiful,
bright, sunny day out,

93
00:03:39,153 --> 00:03:40,755
and what’s this?

94
00:03:40,822 --> 00:03:43,792
Michelle Pfeiffer, Debra Winger
and Holly Hunter

95
00:03:43,858 --> 00:03:45,593
are motioning
for me to come over

96
00:03:45,660 --> 00:03:47,395
and join them
in a game of touch football?

97
00:03:47,462 --> 00:03:48,797
Wow!

98
00:03:48,863 --> 00:03:51,432
They needed a fourth player,
and I just happened along.

99
00:03:51,498 --> 00:03:52,600
Wow!

100
00:03:52,667 --> 00:03:55,970
Hey, that’s Elvis Costello!

101
00:03:56,037 --> 00:03:58,538
He just happened
to walked by, and he's...

102
00:03:58,572 --> 00:04:00,542
Looks like he's asking us
to come over to his house

103
00:04:00,608 --> 00:04:02,843
to hear some new songs
he’s working on.

104
00:04:02,910 --> 00:04:03,978
Wow!

105
00:04:04,045 --> 00:04:05,112
So that's
what would have happened

106
00:04:05,178 --> 00:04:06,713
if I had gone out today.

107
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:08,148
Boy!

108
00:04:08,215 --> 00:04:10,285
I'm really glad I...

109
00:04:10,352 --> 00:04:12,720
stayed inside to...

110
00:04:12,787 --> 00:04:15,790
watch Baywatch.

111
00:04:15,857 --> 00:04:17,625
- Oh. I'll try that.

112
00:04:19,727 --> 00:04:21,162
[ changes channel ]

113
00:04:21,228 --> 00:04:24,132
I’m sitting in a Perkins
looking at a menu.

114
00:04:24,198 --> 00:04:25,633
Well, that doesn't
seem to be

115
00:04:25,700 --> 00:04:27,368
like I’m missing
anything...

116
00:04:27,435 --> 00:04:28,602
Whoa! What?

117
00:04:28,670 --> 00:04:31,405
Oh, Henry Kissinger
and Richard Nixon

118
00:04:31,472 --> 00:04:33,273
are shyly approaching my table.

119
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:36,343
They’re shaking my hand
and patting me on the back.

120
00:04:36,410 --> 00:04:40,415
Henry’s giving me a Kissinger
and Associates sweatshirt.

121
00:04:40,482 --> 00:04:42,150
Nixon, what is he doing?

122
00:04:42,217 --> 00:04:44,418
He’s pointing at the menu.

123
00:04:44,485 --> 00:04:47,155
Ah, he’s recommending
the club sandwich.

124
00:04:47,222 --> 00:04:48,890
[ laughing ]

125
00:04:48,957 --> 00:04:51,492
Oh, I do love this, Frank,

126
00:04:51,558 --> 00:04:53,127
but not as much as I love

127
00:04:53,193 --> 00:04:55,997
sitting at home
watching Baywatch.

128
00:04:56,063 --> 00:04:57,232
[ changes channel ]

129
00:04:57,298 --> 00:04:58,432
- [ on TV ] Oh, no,
I’m in big trouble.

130
00:04:58,498 --> 00:04:59,800
I never should have hung out
with the kids

131
00:04:59,867 --> 00:05:01,502
down by the liquor store.

132
00:05:01,568 --> 00:05:03,237
- Well, sirs,
our invention may look

133
00:05:03,303 --> 00:05:04,572
like just an ordinary
Weber Grill,

134
00:05:04,638 --> 00:05:06,140
but it’s much,
much more than that.

135
00:05:06,207 --> 00:05:08,308
Yes, it’s
the Andrew Lloyd Webber Grill.

136
00:05:08,375 --> 00:05:09,510
[ laughing ]

137
00:05:09,577 --> 00:05:11,278
- Hey, everybody
come and get it.

138
00:05:11,345 --> 00:05:13,982
I've got a whole ream of
Andrew Lloyd Webber scores,

139
00:05:14,048 --> 00:05:15,650
and they’re
all ready to barbecue.

140
00:05:15,717 --> 00:05:17,618
- Yes, now you can take out
your aggression

141
00:05:17,685 --> 00:05:21,022
on the theater world’s most
inescapable troll-like composer.

142
00:05:21,088 --> 00:05:23,423
- Yeah, just stick any
of his many overblown scores

143
00:05:23,490 --> 00:05:24,992
in the Andrew Lloyd Webber
Grill,

144
00:05:25,058 --> 00:05:27,328
and they’ll burn quickly
and efficiently.

145
00:05:27,395 --> 00:05:29,530
- Whether it’s Cats
or Phantom of the Opera.

146
00:05:29,597 --> 00:05:31,265
- Starlight Express
or Evita.

147
00:05:31,332 --> 00:05:32,933
- Hey, wait,
I kind of liked Evita.

148
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:34,368
- No time for sentiment, Joel.

149
00:05:34,435 --> 00:05:36,737
We’ve got to burn
Aspects of Love and Chess.

150
00:05:36,803 --> 00:05:38,272
- Wait a minute.

151
00:05:38,338 --> 00:05:40,508
Andrew Lloyd Webber
didn’t write Chess.

152
00:05:40,575 --> 00:05:42,577
It was Tim Rice
and those guys from ABBA.

153
00:05:42,643 --> 00:05:45,312
- Oh, I think the Andrew Lloyd
Webber Grill can handle them,

154
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:46,980
and we might as well
burn the score

155
00:05:47,048 --> 00:05:48,648
- to Annie while we’re at it.
- Mmm!

156
00:05:48,717 --> 00:05:50,618
- Hey, what
do you think, sirs?

157
00:05:50,685 --> 00:05:52,187
[ Crow and Tom coughing ]

158
00:05:52,253 --> 00:05:56,123
- Oh, man, Elvis is holding up
a picture of Andrew Lloyd Webber

159
00:05:56,190 --> 00:05:57,492
and making fun of it.

160
00:05:57,558 --> 00:05:59,293
Holly, Debra, Michelle, and I

161
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:01,295
are having a good laugh
at his expense.

162
00:06:01,362 --> 00:06:02,897
We’re having a great time.

163
00:06:02,963 --> 00:06:04,632
Why did I stay inside today?

164
00:06:04,698 --> 00:06:06,500
Why, why, why?

165
00:06:06,533 --> 00:06:08,803
Oh, wow,
McKenna’s Gold is on.

166
00:06:08,870 --> 00:06:11,672
- Well, Joel,
your experiment this week

167
00:06:11,738 --> 00:06:13,473
will make you wish you were
singing duets

168
00:06:13,540 --> 00:06:15,743
with Patty LuPone
at Mandy Patinkin’s house.

169
00:06:15,810 --> 00:06:18,847
It's an experiment in padding
from Roger Corman,

170
00:06:18,913 --> 00:06:20,648
and it's called
Swamp Diamonds,

171
00:06:20,715 --> 00:06:24,985
but first a short film
called What To Do On A Date.

172
00:06:25,052 --> 00:06:27,555
You should be able to relate
to that, right, Frank?

173
00:06:27,622 --> 00:06:28,857
- Shh!

174
00:06:28,923 --> 00:06:30,925
- All right,
who wants an overture?

175
00:06:30,992 --> 00:06:32,293
- [ alarm sounding ]
- I do! I do!

176
00:06:32,360 --> 00:06:35,228
- Let's beat it! Ohh!

177
00:06:42,203 --> 00:06:45,573
[ coughing ]

178
00:06:46,973 --> 00:06:50,210
- I need to know
what not to do on a date.

179
00:06:50,277 --> 00:06:52,780
- What, does she have a Ph.D.
in dating?

180
00:06:52,847 --> 00:06:56,917
- Oh, no, this is like having
your mom tell you about sex.

181
00:06:59,487 --> 00:07:01,755
- These two are on a date?
- No, no.

182
00:07:01,822 --> 00:07:04,058
- This is a story about
what to do on a date.

183
00:07:04,125 --> 00:07:05,192
- Goofus and Gallant?

184
00:07:05,258 --> 00:07:07,995
- It begins one early
summer afternoon.

185
00:07:08,062 --> 00:07:09,763
It begins with Jeff.

186
00:07:09,797 --> 00:07:12,667
- Ha! Good one, Jeff.

187
00:07:12,733 --> 00:07:14,268
- And Kay.

188
00:07:14,335 --> 00:07:15,737
- And a human ear.

189
00:07:15,803 --> 00:07:17,003
- And Nick.

190
00:07:17,070 --> 00:07:18,873
- Oh, I meant to do that.

191
00:07:18,940 --> 00:07:21,408
- Bye, Nick.
What a loser.

192
00:07:21,475 --> 00:07:24,312
- Of course, you
may not have a problem

193
00:07:24,378 --> 00:07:25,647
about what to do on a date,

194
00:07:25,713 --> 00:07:29,450
but Nick, well,
he has a real dating problem.

195
00:07:29,517 --> 00:07:33,020
If he took Kay out on a date,
what would they do?

196
00:07:33,087 --> 00:07:34,355
Where would they go?

197
00:07:34,422 --> 00:07:35,623
- [ imitates glass breaking ]

198
00:07:35,690 --> 00:07:36,890
Dennis!

199
00:07:36,957 --> 00:07:39,293
- No, you can’t inject puberty.

200
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:40,862
It has to happen over time.

201
00:07:40,962 --> 00:07:42,463
- Jeff, I’ll get
my tennis racket.

202
00:07:42,530 --> 00:07:43,865
- Okay, Nick, but hurry.

203
00:07:43,932 --> 00:07:45,933
- And, Jeff,
would you quit stalking me?

204
00:07:48,135 --> 00:07:49,570
- Bet he wishes he had a life.

205
00:07:49,637 --> 00:07:51,072
Man, what a loser.

206
00:07:51,138 --> 00:07:52,973
♪ Dubba dubba deeba
dubba dubba ♪

207
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:55,142
Hmm? Oh.

208
00:07:55,208 --> 00:07:57,878
- My God, Kay’s been missing
since last February!

209
00:08:00,080 --> 00:08:02,817
- Hey, what about asking Kay
to come to the community center

210
00:08:02,883 --> 00:08:05,687
with us on Friday to fix up
that scavenger sale?

211
00:08:05,753 --> 00:08:07,153
We could make it
a double date.

212
00:08:07,222 --> 00:08:08,655
- You looked in my book,
didn’t you?

213
00:08:08,722 --> 00:08:09,823
- What?

214
00:08:09,890 --> 00:08:11,492
- You heard what I said.

215
00:08:11,558 --> 00:08:13,560
[ all humming Psycho theme ]

216
00:08:13,627 --> 00:08:16,397
- That's no way
to take a girl on a date,

217
00:08:16,463 --> 00:08:19,367
helping to fix up
the scavenger sale?

218
00:08:19,433 --> 00:08:20,568
Is it?

219
00:08:20,635 --> 00:08:21,668
- Why not?

220
00:08:21,735 --> 00:08:23,703
- On a first date?

221
00:08:23,770 --> 00:08:25,940
- First date?

222
00:08:26,007 --> 00:08:28,675
- Yeah.
Well, Kay’s a swell girl.

223
00:08:28,742 --> 00:08:29,677
- [ groans ]

224
00:08:29,743 --> 00:08:32,780
- I was thinking
of calling her,

225
00:08:32,847 --> 00:08:35,048
maybe ask her
to the show Friday.

226
00:08:35,115 --> 00:08:36,483
Good picture, Wagon Train.

227
00:08:36,550 --> 00:08:37,852
- [ snickers ]

228
00:08:37,918 --> 00:08:39,587
- Well, hurry up and call her
and let’s get going.

229
00:08:39,653 --> 00:08:41,055
I still have
to get my things yet.

230
00:08:41,122 --> 00:08:42,590
- Call her now?

231
00:08:42,657 --> 00:08:45,458
- Sure. You know
the number, don't you?

232
00:08:47,228 --> 00:08:48,662
- Okay.

233
00:08:48,728 --> 00:08:51,932
- But I’ve got
a Forever Plaid audition.

234
00:08:51,998 --> 00:08:54,768
- I’m kidding, I’m kidding.
Hang up the phone.

235
00:08:54,835 --> 00:08:57,405
- With each dial,
he comes closer and closer

236
00:08:57,472 --> 00:08:59,507
to the most humiliating moment
of his life.

237
00:09:01,575 --> 00:09:02,843
- What if she says no?

238
00:09:02,910 --> 00:09:03,945
- She will.

239
00:09:04,012 --> 00:09:05,913
- Hello?
- Oh, hello, Kay?

240
00:09:05,980 --> 00:09:06,913
- No.

241
00:09:06,980 --> 00:09:07,915
- This is Nick Baxter.

242
00:09:07,982 --> 00:09:08,917
- No.

243
00:09:08,983 --> 00:09:11,418
- Oh, hello, Nick,
how are you?

244
00:09:11,485 --> 00:09:12,420
- I hurt inside.

245
00:09:12,487 --> 00:09:13,620
- Fine, thanks.

246
00:09:13,687 --> 00:09:15,957
Say, Kay, next Friday,

247
00:09:16,023 --> 00:09:18,325
would you like to see
Wagon Train at the movies?

248
00:09:18,358 --> 00:09:19,593
- I didn’t think so, bye.

249
00:09:19,660 --> 00:09:20,995
- Oh, I’m sorry Nick.

250
00:09:21,062 --> 00:09:24,032
I suppose you’ve got
your mind made up to see it?

251
00:09:24,098 --> 00:09:26,067
- You’ve seen it?

252
00:09:26,133 --> 00:09:27,100
She’s already seen it.

253
00:09:27,167 --> 00:09:28,202
- Hang up.

254
00:09:28,268 --> 00:09:29,770
- Well, ask her
about the scavenger sale.

255
00:09:29,837 --> 00:09:32,105
Go ahead.

256
00:09:32,172 --> 00:09:35,042
- Uh, Kay, you wouldn't
want to help

257
00:09:35,108 --> 00:09:36,543
get the scavenger sale ready

258
00:09:36,610 --> 00:09:39,047
at the community center,
would you?

259
00:09:39,113 --> 00:09:40,447
- How many ways can I say no?

260
00:09:40,513 --> 00:09:41,948
- Oh, I’ve been hearing
about that.

261
00:09:42,015 --> 00:09:43,883
Yes, I’d like to very much.

262
00:09:43,952 --> 00:09:45,485
- Is it okay if I bring
my boyfriend, Dave?

263
00:09:45,552 --> 00:09:46,553
- You would?

264
00:09:46,620 --> 00:09:48,255
All right.

265
00:09:48,322 --> 00:09:51,025
I’ll call for you then
about 7:00?

266
00:09:51,092 --> 00:09:52,460
Bye.

267
00:09:52,527 --> 00:09:54,362
- I better change my pants.

268
00:09:54,428 --> 00:09:57,465
- I sure didn’t think she’d go
to a place like that for a date.

269
00:09:57,532 --> 00:09:59,567
- With a loser like me.

270
00:09:59,633 --> 00:10:00,968
- Where’s my racket?

271
00:10:01,035 --> 00:10:02,337
- I should spank myself.

272
00:10:02,403 --> 00:10:04,205
- Oh, ready?

273
00:10:04,272 --> 00:10:06,040
- You know, you better think
some more, fellow,

274
00:10:06,107 --> 00:10:07,273
about what to do on a date.

275
00:10:07,342 --> 00:10:08,675
- She said yes, okay?

276
00:10:08,742 --> 00:10:10,645
- You think she’ll
have a good time?

277
00:10:10,712 --> 00:10:12,347
What do you
suppose I’ll have to do?

278
00:10:15,215 --> 00:10:17,183
- Oh, yeah, who wouldn’t
have a good time?

279
00:10:17,250 --> 00:10:18,685
[ jaunty music ]

280
00:10:18,752 --> 00:10:22,222
- ♪ June is bustin' out
all over ♪

281
00:10:25,092 --> 00:10:27,060
- Aaron Copeland
throws a party.

282
00:10:28,462 --> 00:10:31,265
- Now, you may not have thought
of this sort of thing

283
00:10:31,332 --> 00:10:33,000
as a place to take a date,

284
00:10:33,067 --> 00:10:34,735
but it looks like it
could be fun.

285
00:10:34,802 --> 00:10:38,205
- Doesn't it?! Doesn’t it?!

286
00:10:38,272 --> 00:10:40,875
- Boy, this is fun,
with the used lamps

287
00:10:40,942 --> 00:10:43,177
and the festoonery. Whoa.

288
00:10:43,243 --> 00:10:45,980
- This party is getting
out of control.

289
00:10:46,047 --> 00:10:49,083
- A group doing things together.
- Herds.

290
00:10:49,150 --> 00:10:51,485
- Pretty good idea
for a first date.

291
00:10:51,552 --> 00:10:54,388
Plenty to do, plenty of people
to talk to.

292
00:10:54,455 --> 00:10:57,323
- Plenty of opportunities
to screw up big time.

293
00:10:57,392 --> 00:11:00,060
- But let’s see if Nick and Kay
are enjoying it.

294
00:11:00,127 --> 00:11:03,697
- No amount of dust could make
this thing look any older.

295
00:11:03,763 --> 00:11:06,067
- Boy, that is an old-timer.

296
00:11:06,133 --> 00:11:07,902
- And the dust comment

297
00:11:07,968 --> 00:11:10,237
proves to be a major
miscalculation on Kay’s part.

298
00:11:10,303 --> 00:11:11,405
- "Boy, that is an old-timer."

299
00:11:11,472 --> 00:11:14,742
What was I thinking?
Stupid, stupid, stupid.

300
00:11:14,808 --> 00:11:16,710
- Kay’s worked
on the kill floor.

301
00:11:16,777 --> 00:11:18,678
She knows
where to deliver the blow.

302
00:11:18,745 --> 00:11:21,348
- Can you help me
put this sign up?

303
00:11:21,415 --> 00:11:22,348
- Oh, sure.

304
00:11:22,417 --> 00:11:23,583
- Sure, I’ll do everything.

305
00:11:23,650 --> 00:11:25,352
This relationship
is suffocating me.

306
00:11:25,452 --> 00:11:28,955
- I couldn't quite reach
that far.

307
00:11:29,022 --> 00:11:30,357
- How’s this?

308
00:11:30,423 --> 00:11:32,727
- Looks a bit gangly, oaf.

309
00:11:32,793 --> 00:11:33,793
- Yeah.

310
00:11:33,860 --> 00:11:36,030
- Thanks.

311
00:11:36,097 --> 00:11:37,898
- Ooh! Ow! Son of a--Mmm!

312
00:11:37,965 --> 00:11:39,100
Ooh ooh!

313
00:11:39,167 --> 00:11:40,802
- Say, Kay.

314
00:11:40,868 --> 00:11:41,868
- Will you marry me?

315
00:11:41,935 --> 00:11:43,403
- Are you really having
a good time?

316
00:11:43,470 --> 00:11:44,638
- Of course, I am.

317
00:11:44,705 --> 00:11:46,473
I’m glad you thought
of coming here.

318
00:11:46,540 --> 00:11:47,875
- So am I.

319
00:11:47,942 --> 00:11:50,978
- I remember
when I was that age.

320
00:11:51,045 --> 00:11:52,847
- Yes, this was a good idea.

321
00:11:52,913 --> 00:11:56,917
- Oh, Kay, how about helping
with refreshments for the day?

322
00:11:56,983 --> 00:11:57,952
- All right.

323
00:11:58,018 --> 00:11:59,120
- For the Crips or the Bloods?

324
00:11:59,187 --> 00:12:02,890
- See you later.
- Oh, yeah, sure.

325
00:12:02,957 --> 00:12:04,925
- Sorry it didn’t work out.

326
00:12:04,992 --> 00:12:07,060
- Got any more tacks?

327
00:12:07,127 --> 00:12:09,028
- I think they’re over there
by the door.

328
00:12:10,397 --> 00:12:12,065
- I got to cut him loose.

329
00:12:13,767 --> 00:12:16,070
- Yes, it was a good idea
to come here.

330
00:12:16,137 --> 00:12:17,237
- At first.

331
00:12:17,303 --> 00:12:19,140
- How does Jeff get ideas
like that?

332
00:12:19,207 --> 00:12:21,075
- From the voices inside
his head.

333
00:12:21,142 --> 00:12:22,877
- Hey, what’s this?

334
00:12:22,943 --> 00:12:25,578
Maybe this is where Jeff
gets ideas.

335
00:12:25,645 --> 00:12:26,947
- The lunch menu?

336
00:12:27,013 --> 00:12:29,283
- A list
of coming activities.

337
00:12:29,350 --> 00:12:33,153
A bike trip--
that could be a lot of fun.

338
00:12:33,220 --> 00:12:36,490
A chance to do things
with a group to get acquainted.

339
00:12:36,557 --> 00:12:37,925
- Now, is this fantasy?

340
00:12:37,992 --> 00:12:40,828
- It does look like a good time
if your partner likes it.

341
00:12:40,895 --> 00:12:42,195
- That’s a big "if."

342
00:12:42,262 --> 00:12:44,063
One bad idea could mean the end
of your relationship.

343
00:12:44,132 --> 00:12:46,200
- Would Kay enjoy a bike trip?

344
00:12:46,267 --> 00:12:47,802
Or a weenie roast?

345
00:12:47,868 --> 00:12:50,070
- Nick, no!

346
00:12:50,137 --> 00:12:53,573
- Another group date,
a chance to learn

347
00:12:53,640 --> 00:12:56,243
the give and take of working
and playing together.

348
00:12:56,310 --> 00:12:58,145
Not much arranging needed,

349
00:12:58,212 --> 00:13:01,582
not much expense,
and that can be important.

350
00:13:01,682 --> 00:13:04,352
Could be a fine date
if Kay would like it.

351
00:13:04,418 --> 00:13:06,087
- Kay, Kay, Kay!
What about my needs?

352
00:13:06,153 --> 00:13:07,955
- ...go to a baseball game,

353
00:13:08,022 --> 00:13:10,290
a date
that’s not too involved,

354
00:13:10,357 --> 00:13:12,493
one you can carry
through comfortably.

355
00:13:12,560 --> 00:13:14,295
- Oops, Nick strikes out.

356
00:13:14,362 --> 00:13:15,795
- And there are all sorts
of other sports events,

357
00:13:15,863 --> 00:13:18,665
inexpensive dates
most of them.

358
00:13:18,732 --> 00:13:20,433
Swimming meets, for example.

359
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:23,770
- Are very dull.

360
00:13:23,837 --> 00:13:26,007
- You can find many good ideas
for dates.

361
00:13:26,073 --> 00:13:28,442
- Hey, hey,
our luggage is here! Whoo!

362
00:13:28,508 --> 00:13:31,245
- Dates that give you a good
chance to get acquainted...

363
00:13:33,313 --> 00:13:34,848
- and dates that you don't...
- Heh heh heh, yeah!

364
00:13:34,915 --> 00:13:36,783
- ...have to worry
too much about in advance.

365
00:13:36,850 --> 00:13:38,418
- Nick, snap out of it!

366
00:13:38,485 --> 00:13:40,888
- It was a good deal
coming here tonight,

367
00:13:40,955 --> 00:13:42,957
double dating
with Jeff and Phyllis.

368
00:13:43,023 --> 00:13:45,192
- But mostly Jeff.

369
00:13:45,258 --> 00:13:49,230
- When all four are good friends
and enjoy the same things,

370
00:13:49,297 --> 00:13:51,798
double dates
can be lots of fun.

371
00:13:51,865 --> 00:13:54,768
Or you can ask Kay to go on
dates with you alone

372
00:13:54,835 --> 00:13:57,270
if you plan for what she enjoys.

373
00:13:57,337 --> 00:13:58,638
- [ humming adult film music ]

374
00:13:58,705 --> 00:14:00,540
- Hey, you loafer.

375
00:14:00,607 --> 00:14:02,543
What are you doing over there?

376
00:14:02,610 --> 00:14:04,745
- Oh, thought you were talking
to me, sorry.

377
00:14:04,845 --> 00:14:07,280
Awkward.

378
00:14:07,347 --> 00:14:08,748
- Fine thing.

379
00:14:08,815 --> 00:14:10,817
As soon as Kay isn't
around to supervise,

380
00:14:10,885 --> 00:14:12,385
you go off on a vacation.

381
00:14:12,452 --> 00:14:13,687
- You know, maestro,

382
00:14:13,753 --> 00:14:16,290
I don’t think your date ideas
are so original.

383
00:14:16,357 --> 00:14:17,792
- Hmm?
- What?

384
00:14:17,858 --> 00:14:21,262
- About what to do
on a date, I mean.

385
00:14:21,328 --> 00:14:23,030
- Ah, sorry.

386
00:14:23,097 --> 00:14:26,333
- What I'd like to know is,
how can you pick out

387
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:28,402
what a girl would like to do?

388
00:14:28,468 --> 00:14:30,637
A certain girl, I mean.

389
00:14:30,703 --> 00:14:32,740
- Well, you might ask her.

390
00:14:32,807 --> 00:14:34,675
- Oh, I should
have thought of that.

391
00:14:34,742 --> 00:14:37,177
- Yeah, Nick’s looking for you.

392
00:14:37,243 --> 00:14:38,245
- Jeez, hide me.

393
00:14:38,312 --> 00:14:39,513
- Is he good company?

394
00:14:39,580 --> 00:14:41,182
He always seems so quiet.

395
00:14:41,248 --> 00:14:43,017
- We get along all right.

396
00:14:43,083 --> 00:14:44,652
He's got
a good sense of humor,

397
00:14:44,718 --> 00:14:46,787
and he hasn’t run
out of things to say yet.

398
00:14:46,853 --> 00:14:47,788
- He will.

399
00:14:47,855 --> 00:14:48,788
- He’s kind of cute.

400
00:14:48,855 --> 00:14:50,257
You going to date him again?

401
00:14:50,323 --> 00:14:51,725
- I don’t know.

402
00:14:51,792 --> 00:14:54,428
Besides, he hasn’t asked me.

403
00:14:54,495 --> 00:14:55,595
George, get out of there.

404
00:14:55,662 --> 00:14:57,030
- I’m hungry.

405
00:14:57,097 --> 00:14:59,267
- If you want to do something,
take the Cokes out there.

406
00:14:59,333 --> 00:15:01,468
- Okay.
- All set?

407
00:15:01,535 --> 00:15:02,870
- I think so.

408
00:15:02,937 --> 00:15:04,905
- Why can’t Nick be fun
like George is?

409
00:15:04,972 --> 00:15:06,473
- Come and get it, everybody!

410
00:15:06,540 --> 00:15:08,208
- Come on, we eat!

411
00:15:08,308 --> 00:15:09,877
- Eat at your own risk.

412
00:15:09,943 --> 00:15:12,613
- The sensuous pagan ritual
begins.

413
00:15:12,680 --> 00:15:14,147
- Mmm!

414
00:15:14,213 --> 00:15:15,582
- Hi, stranger.

415
00:15:15,648 --> 00:15:17,583
- I decided to come
out of hiding.

416
00:15:17,652 --> 00:15:19,420
- Is she Salman Rushdie?

417
00:15:19,487 --> 00:15:21,122
- You make the sandwiches?

418
00:15:21,188 --> 00:15:23,223
- Nah, just the Cokes.

419
00:15:23,290 --> 00:15:25,592
- Nick, other people
have to eat, too.

420
00:15:25,658 --> 00:15:29,128
[ all talking at once ]

421
00:15:34,368 --> 00:15:37,137
- Oh, Nick, you take me
to the best places.

422
00:15:37,203 --> 00:15:40,040
- Oh, oh, Nick's got back.

423
00:15:40,073 --> 00:15:41,508
- Your table, Madame.

424
00:15:41,575 --> 00:15:42,977
- Thank you, sir.

425
00:15:43,043 --> 00:15:44,645
- Boy, they’re a regular
Tracy and Hepburn up there.

426
00:15:44,712 --> 00:15:46,513
Look at them up there.

427
00:15:46,580 --> 00:15:48,082
- This is fun, isn’t it?

428
00:15:48,148 --> 00:15:51,118
- Yeah, you sure you like to do
things like this?

429
00:15:51,185 --> 00:15:52,185
- Sure.

430
00:15:52,252 --> 00:15:53,353
- What do you want
from me, Nick?

431
00:15:53,420 --> 00:15:55,355
- I thought girls wanted
fellas to take them

432
00:15:55,422 --> 00:15:57,725
to fancy places,
spend lots of money.

433
00:15:57,792 --> 00:16:00,493
- Not this girl.

434
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:04,598
- You know, I sort of
like things like this, too.

435
00:16:04,665 --> 00:16:08,435
You won’t laugh, I like to go
on bicycle trips, too.

436
00:16:08,502 --> 00:16:09,437
[ all laughing ]

437
00:16:09,503 --> 00:16:10,570
Do you?

438
00:16:10,637 --> 00:16:12,840
- And miniature golf.
Do you like that?

439
00:16:12,907 --> 00:16:15,342
- Yeah, and weenie roasts
and square dances.

440
00:16:15,408 --> 00:16:17,945
- And baseball games
and taffy pulls.

441
00:16:18,012 --> 00:16:19,413
I think they’re swell.

442
00:16:19,480 --> 00:16:22,348
- Say, you like to do
lots of things, don’t you?

443
00:16:22,415 --> 00:16:24,283
- Hey, you read the list, too.

444
00:16:24,352 --> 00:16:28,022
- Say, Kay, next Friday, there's
going to be a weenie roast,

445
00:16:28,088 --> 00:16:30,890
or there will be a bowling party
if you’d rather do that.

446
00:16:30,957 --> 00:16:31,958
- It won’t work, Nick.

447
00:16:32,025 --> 00:16:33,360
- Or we could go
to the band concert.

448
00:16:33,427 --> 00:16:36,297
- Well, I’d enjoy going
to the weenie roast very much.

449
00:16:36,363 --> 00:16:37,397
- With someone else.

450
00:16:37,463 --> 00:16:38,598
- Unless you’d
rather go bowling.

451
00:16:38,665 --> 00:16:40,267
- No, I’d rather go
to the weenie roast.

452
00:16:40,333 --> 00:16:41,935
- Let’s not fight, Nick.
It’s our first date.

453
00:16:42,002 --> 00:16:44,538
- If Jeff goes with Phyllis,
we could double-date with them.

454
00:16:44,572 --> 00:16:46,240
- Hey, Nick, come on!

455
00:16:46,307 --> 00:16:47,607
We’re going to break open
the ice cream!

456
00:16:47,673 --> 00:16:48,708
- Okay, be right there!

457
00:16:48,775 --> 00:16:50,677
- Ice cream?
I love this party!

458
00:16:50,743 --> 00:16:52,145
- So we're on again
on Friday?

459
00:16:52,212 --> 00:16:55,115
- Yes, Nick, it’s a date.

460
00:16:55,182 --> 00:16:57,617
- Hey, guys,
what’s a weenie roast?

461
00:16:57,683 --> 00:17:00,320
- Yes, there are lots of things
to do on dates

462
00:17:00,387 --> 00:17:02,555
if you know how to look
for them,

463
00:17:02,622 --> 00:17:05,258
if you plan them with
the other person in mind,

464
00:17:05,325 --> 00:17:09,797
and if you really try to make
sure each date’s a good time.

465
00:17:09,863 --> 00:17:11,965
If you do these things,

466
00:17:12,032 --> 00:17:14,100
you’ll know
what to do on your date.

467
00:17:14,167 --> 00:17:16,970
- But if you're an AV geek,
don't even kid yourself.

468
00:17:17,003 --> 00:17:17,938
[ laughing ]

469
00:17:18,005 --> 00:17:19,940
- Had this been
an actual date,

470
00:17:20,007 --> 00:17:22,008
you would have been
instructed where to go.

471
00:17:30,917 --> 00:17:32,085
- Huh.

472
00:17:32,152 --> 00:17:33,987
So I guess the sculptors
and the painters

473
00:17:34,053 --> 00:17:35,522
got together
and did this thing.

474
00:17:35,588 --> 00:17:36,690
- Yeah, but
you got to watch out

475
00:17:36,757 --> 00:17:38,992
for those Axis Artists,
you know? Yeah.

476
00:17:39,058 --> 00:17:41,628
[ somber woodwinds playing ]

477
00:17:43,197 --> 00:17:47,433
- The bassoon will be played
by Marie Windsor.

478
00:17:47,500 --> 00:17:50,137
- ♪ Oh, swamp diamond ♪

479
00:17:50,203 --> 00:17:53,340
[ imitating electric guitar ]

480
00:17:54,708 --> 00:17:57,677
- Boy, really
perky music here, huh?

481
00:17:57,743 --> 00:17:58,912
- Whoo!

482
00:17:58,978 --> 00:18:02,615
- Oh, Touch Conners?
I don’t think I...

483
00:18:02,682 --> 00:18:05,852
- The credits are done
by a black velvet artist.

484
00:18:05,918 --> 00:18:07,253
- Neal Claudel.

485
00:18:07,320 --> 00:18:09,990
- Introducing and
saying good-bye to Jil Jarmyn.

486
00:18:11,858 --> 00:18:14,060
[ music crescendos ]

487
00:18:16,028 --> 00:18:19,365
- Is that supposed to be
an example of my headache pains?

488
00:18:19,432 --> 00:18:21,835
- Night Gallery,
the lost episodes.

489
00:18:26,740 --> 00:18:29,142
- [ humming to film music ]

490
00:18:29,208 --> 00:18:31,912
[ imitating diving,
strafing warplane ]

491
00:18:35,282 --> 00:18:38,685
- Oh, I know, these are J.J.’s
Good Times paintings.

492
00:18:38,752 --> 00:18:40,353
- Oh!

493
00:18:42,255 --> 00:18:44,123
- Women in Cell Block H.

494
00:18:47,360 --> 00:18:50,830
- I feel like I should take
Prozac after hearing this music.

495
00:18:54,935 --> 00:18:56,337
- Good ol' Roger Corman.

496
00:18:56,403 --> 00:18:57,838
This is going
to go down hard, guys.

497
00:18:57,905 --> 00:18:59,640
- Mm-hmm.

498
00:18:59,707 --> 00:19:03,577
- To be like the Cor-man,
to live like the Cor-man.

499
00:19:03,643 --> 00:19:05,445
- This is a Pink Panther movie.

500
00:19:07,247 --> 00:19:09,783
- Well, whatever it is,
it’s got pretty lousy animation.

501
00:19:11,418 --> 00:19:13,287
- [ jazz playing ]
- Hmm?

502
00:19:15,655 --> 00:19:18,592
- Apparently they’ve seen
the "How To Date" short.

503
00:19:18,658 --> 00:19:21,495
- Well, there goes
the PTA conga line.

504
00:19:21,562 --> 00:19:23,963
[ humming conga music ]

505
00:19:26,433 --> 00:19:28,768
- That’s not Touch Conners,
that’s Mike Connors!

506
00:19:28,835 --> 00:19:30,337
